WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.839
Film Courage: Ken, as a tenured professor
in your mid-40s, what made you think you could

00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:07.839
change careers?

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:12.679
Dr. Ken Atchity, producer and author: Well
this is America and you can do whatever you

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:13.679
want to do.

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:18.330
It’s one of the great things about this
country.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.510
What I was doing was very related to a career
I’m in now.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:28.150
It was developing stories, developing writers,
and of course teaching a number of things

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:36.810
that I no longer teach like classical literature
and Italian literature and I do miss that

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:40.990
part but it was basically an extension of
what I’ve been doing all of my life which

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:47.340
is developing stories, analyzing stories,
publishing stories, helping people publish

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:51.100
stories, and now, getting stories produced
into movies.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.440
So it’s all united by storytelling.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:01:02.910
I had no idea which world was sort of the
bigger world of ideas, the world of academia

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:09.040
that I had been in for 17 years or the world
I went into and I discovered that the world

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.920
I went into was really the world of ideas
because it’s a world in which people are

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:21.750
tracking ideas across continents to find out
who owns the rights to a story.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:27.840
They pay lots of money to acquire the story
(at least they used to pay lots of money)

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:33.840
and they spend millions of dollars to turn
the story into a movie and they’re fiercely

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.950
competitive about the world of ideas.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:43.520
The motion picture business is the jungle
of ideas and it’s survival of the best idea

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.800
and the best business people.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:49.040
I always so it’s called Show Business for
a reason.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:55.950
It’s not just about show, it’s about the
business of how stories get developed into

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.440
movies that the whole world can see.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:05.049
Film Courage: I’m hoping we can go back
to maybe before you made this transition to

00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:07.780
wanting to be in film?

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:12.280
Was there something that happened, was there
a time in your life that where you felt like

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:13.280
“You know what?

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:14.520
I want a new challenge.”

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:21.209
Dr. Ken Atchity: That’s a good question
because I’ve reflected on it all of my life

00:02:21.209 --> 00:02:26.910
since then and it was actually provoked by
my receiving tenure.

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:34.170
I actually belonged to an untenured faculty
committee against tenure and one day when

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:40.390
I was a Fulbright professor in Bologna, Italy,
I got a telegraph from the Dean of the faculty

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:45.220
at Occidental College telling me that I’d
received tenure in my absence.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:50.960
And my reaction to it was not very understandable
to my friends and colleagues.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.470
I became deeply depressed for about a year.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:03:01.080
And it took me a long time to figure out why
I was depressed and it was because I had really

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:07.160
never asked to be in this golden cage where
nothing can happen to you.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:14.780
It was like the most secure place you could
be and I realized at the time that my father’s

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.670
chief value in life was security.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:22.140
He was a child of The Depression and security
was all important to him.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:26.000
And I had to admit to myself that it wasn’t
that important to me.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.620
I never worried about being secure.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:34.190
I’d published lots of things and I was in
demand as a speaker and just never had to

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:35.950
worry about it.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:42.000
And what my value was freedom and I didn’t
feel and I didn’t feel freedom when under

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.070
a structure where you had to behave a certain
way and you had to know a year in advance

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:53.030
that on the week of October 12th you’d be
teaching the 8th book of The Iliad.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:58.271
And it was wonderful to be teaching The Iliad
but that to know a year in advance you were

00:03:58.271 --> 00:03:59.660
going to be somewhere.

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:02.519
I now live in a world where I don’t know
where I am going to be tomorrow literally

00:04:02.519 --> 00:04:07.520
and it’s complete opposite, it’s a free
world.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:13.830
And of course I realized that as I got older
that freedom is as much an illusion as security

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:18.000
(both of them are illusions), but it was my
illusion.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.889
Security was not my illusion and so I’ve
lived with complete insecurity but with the

00:04:24.889 --> 00:04:29.680
freedom to express myself creatively and in
every possible way which is what the film

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:35.999
business allows me to do and so that was very
exciting to me.

00:04:35.999 --> 00:04:39.659
Film Courage: And do you ever tell people
that?

00:04:39.659 --> 00:04:45.210
If they are looking to be in a creative pursuit
whether it’s being an author or screenwriter

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:51.669
or actor, that security will probably be something
that they will not encounter and to be okay

00:04:51.669 --> 00:04:52.669
with that?

00:04:52.669 --> 00:04:53.669
Dr. Ken Atchity: Absolutely.

00:04:53.669 --> 00:04:56.620
I mean this is not a career to wish on anyone.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:03.590
You have to have a burning desire to do it
and you have to be willing to sacrifice anything

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:10.300
to do it and to persist despite every setback
and I can tell you that this is a business

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:15.659
in which, a career in which this never gets
easier, I don’t care how many movies you’ve

00:05:15.659 --> 00:05:16.659
done.

00:05:16.659 --> 00:05:21.849
The next one is going to be the biggest challenge
you’ve ever faced, the world changes all

00:05:21.849 --> 00:05:22.849
the time.

00:05:22.849 --> 00:05:28.539
It’s been changing ever since I’ve been
in it which is around 30 years now and it

00:05:28.539 --> 00:05:35.930
never gets any easier and it never gets any
more secure and even if you’ve had windfalls

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:42.449
and lots of money you would, you would put
it into your next project because people in

00:05:42.449 --> 00:05:50.650
this business believe in what they are doing,
that’s their most important common belief,

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:56.330
you can see it at The Academy Awards, you
hear the stories as they received rewards

00:05:56.330 --> 00:06:02.110
that they never thought they’d get or been
waiting for a lifetime for, they all have

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:08.930
one thing in common, the ability to sacrifice
what everyone else considers the most important

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:16.580
things in order to achieve the dream of getting
the story told to the whole world and that’s

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:17.770
the great thing about the career.

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:23.569
There is no limits to it, it’s infinitely
challenging, it’s constantly challenging,

00:06:23.569 --> 00:06:29.830
there are surprises every day and it’s completely
unpredictable.

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:36.009
Film Courage: I’m almost thinking of a sales
position, like door-to-door where you have

00:06:36.009 --> 00:06:46.580
to just suit-up and work your farm and it
sounds like with this industry it’s an everyday

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:51.259
sort of you have to be that person drumming
up leads and things like that?

00:06:51.259 --> 00:06:55.300
Dr. Ken Atchity: Yeah, it’s a completely
self-starting business.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:07:03.069
You hear repeatedly from actors and from writers
and everyone that being represented by an

00:07:03.069 --> 00:07:09.999
agency does not really help because everyone
said “I’ve always gotten my best jobs

00:07:09.999 --> 00:07:10.999
myself.”

00:07:10.999 --> 00:07:16.449
And I hear that from musicians and from every
member of this business that they get their

00:07:16.449 --> 00:07:25.530
own work and suiting up every morning is putting
on your brain and telling it that it’s got

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:33.460
to be happy and go out there and it doesn’t
matter how bad things are in reality, you

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:38.610
have to put on a happy face and I have gone
to many meetings that I absolutely did not

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:43.559
feel like going to because something had just
happened that was a setback and I just thought

00:07:43.559 --> 00:07:47.929
I want to stay home and lick my wounds, but
I go to the meeting and it turns out that

00:07:47.929 --> 00:07:52.900
those meetings are the best meetings that
you can go to and when you walk out you think

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:53.900
“Thank God I went!

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:56.460
I mean what would have happened if I hadn’t
gone?”

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:05.169
And that is suiting up, that is definitely
a kind of nightly encounter to put on your

00:08:05.169 --> 00:08:10.149
armor and go out there and you are working
with people who are doing the same thing and

00:08:10.149 --> 00:08:14.379
that’s part of the exhilaration to it is
that you know that the person sitting across

00:08:14.379 --> 00:08:20.080
from you may have had worse things happen
to him in the last 24 hours but he’s still

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:26.080
there on the job and putting on a happy face
and getting it done.

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:32.010
It’s very deceptive and seductive when people
come here for the first time especially clients

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:38.860
of mine, I warn them that they will be experiencing
what’s called The Development Dance where

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:43.810
everyone will be extremely nice to them and
extremely positive and then they’ll never

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:50.080
hear from them again and that’s because
people are doing their job and their job is

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:57.000
to find out what this person has to offer
the world and if it’s extremely exciting

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:02.040
(which is rare, very rare), then you’ll
hear from them.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:08.930
But most of the time it’s not extremely
exciting and exciting enough and/or it doesn’t

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:14.340
fit the agenda of this person’s company
at the moment, they have something too much

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:19.590
like it in development or they have a boss
who does not want to do that particular kind

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:22.470
of thing, etc.

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:27.090
But their job is to be the best audience possible
for any story that comes along that could

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:34.930
be a dramatic story that people would attract
audiences and so they’ll be happy in the

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:39.650
meeting and then at the end of the meeting
(you know, behind the back of the person who

00:09:39.650 --> 00:09:44.290
came to the meeting) they’ll make a decision
about whether to pursue it or not and that’s

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:45.570
what you’re up against.

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:47.320
So you’re like a door-to-door salesman.

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:51.470
I always say there’s a great blackboard
in the sky that has every “no” you’ll

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:58.250
ever receive in your life written on it and
finally a “yes” at the end of the “no’s”

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:02.570
and the only catch is, you can’t see it
so what do you do?

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:04.680
You go through the “no’s” as fast as
you can.

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:10.910
That’s the only way to deal with that blackboard
and that’s what successful people do in

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:13.829
the business, they just keep getting those
“no’s” until somebody says “yes."

